   187	## Standing design rules: section 0, the paid-for rules (spec section 4; standing-rules.md)
   188	
   189	Six design rules this project has already paid for with reproduced defects. They
   190	bind every phase. A spec discharges each by name (the specific loader that must
   191	be total, the specific layer that gets a time-bound test, the specific policy
   192	walk that must not sprout per-kind copies) or argues it inapplicable in one
   193	sentence.
   194	
   195	### 1. Totality belongs to the function, not the caller
   196	
   197	"This only ever sees validated documents" is a claim about today's callers.
   198	Any new consumer of document shapes is **total over arbitrary JSON**, with a
   199	hostile-input test. The defect that bought this rule: a gate that consumed
   200	proposal-supplied bytes with no validation layer in front of it, in a codebase
   201	where every other consumer had one.
   202	
   203	Totality is more than catching one exception class. Check all of:
   204	
   205	- The cap is applied to the **read**, not after it. A cap applied after
   206	  `read_bytes()` is a report, not a cap.
   207	- Exception classes that are not the obvious one: a very long integer literal
   208	  raises `ValueError`, not `JSONDecodeError`; deep nesting raises
   209	  `RecursionError`.
   210	- The non-JSON constants. `NaN` and infinities are accepted by default and are
   211	  not JSON.
   212	- The docstring. If it promises totality, a traceback out of that function is a
   213	  contradiction of the shipped contract, not a rough edge.
   214	
   215	### 2. An input cap bounds the document, not the algorithm
   216	
   217	A rule quadratic in a count the document controls turns a legal input into
   218	minutes of CPU under the cap. Every phase that adds work proportional to
   219	untrusted input ships a **time-bound test over the layer it adds**. This is the
   220	class that was caught once and that nobody searched for twice.
   221	
   222	### 3. Validation order is part of the design, stated per boundary
   223	
   224	Which check runs first is a design decision with a user-visible consequence: a
   225	gated delta should report "gated", not "your candidate is invalid", or the author
   226	is sent into the other half of the same gate. Where two orderings both reject and
   227	no return value distinguishes them, **pin the order by source-order assertion**.
   228	
   229	### 4. A policy governing N element kinds is enforced by one walk over all N
   230	
   231	Not N copies of the policy. A shared policy implemented per-kind will be right in
   232	the kinds someone remembered and absent in the rest. The corollary bites on the
   233	fix side too: when **one** function serves both an authoring-time warning and an
   234	evaluation-time verdict, a defect in it is wrong in both places and is fixed
   235	once, which is the point.
   236	
   237	### 5. Normative prose has exactly one home
   238	
   239	A document written alongside its implementation is a second chance to make the
   240	same mistake, not independent evidence. Keep each normative sentence in one home
   241	so a divergence is a **bug** rather than an ambiguity; everything else cites it.
   242	When a phase's work makes two homes disagree, one of them yields in that phase,
   243	and the spec names which.
   244	
   245	**Scope:** the rule governs normative *prose*. It does not forbid byte-identical
   246	sync-pinned copies of an interchange schema: a canonical reference plus a
   247	vendored copy held identical by a mechanical identity test is ONE artifact. This
   248	scope is written down because a reviewer reading the unscoped rule literally
   249	reported such a pair as a violation.
   250	
   251	### 6. Resolve contested representation questions by measurement, not argument
   252	
   253	Where the codebase can host an experiment, run it. A contested digest question
   254	was settled by making the change and watching eight committed-payload tests go
   255	red: the artifact's own contract decided it, in minutes, with no debate.
